        title: "The FMLA and job-protected leave",
        explanation:
          "The Family and Medical Leave Act entitles an eligible employee to twelve weeks of unpaid, job-protected leave in a twelve-month period for the birth or adoption of a child, to care for a spouse, child, or parent with a serious health condition, or for the employee's own serious health condition. Eligibility itself is narrower than most employees assume: the employer must have fifty or more employees within seventy-five miles, and the employee must have worked for that employer for at least twelve months and at least 1,250 hours in the preceding twelve-month period, a threshold that excludes a meaningful share of part-time and recently hired workers entirely. On return from qualifying leave, the employee is generally entitled to reinstatement to the same position or an equivalent one, meaning substantially similar duties, pay, benefits, and status, not merely the same title. The FMLA interacts closely with the ADA where a serious health condition is also a disability: leave beyond the twelve-week FMLA entitlement can itself become a required reasonable accommodation under the ADA if it would not impose an undue hardship, meaning the two statutes are frequently analyzed together rather than as independent, self-contained protections.",
