    connections:
      "Ten ideas, one framework, moving from the individual employee outward to the workplace as a whole. At-will employment is the default every other doctrine in this lecture modifies: Title VII, the ADA, and the FMLA each carve out a specific, statutorily defined zone where the default no longer applies, protecting against discrimination, denying accommodation, or denying leave, respectively. Harassment and retaliation then police how an employer actually handles a complaint once one is made, whether about discrimination or about anything else covered in this lecture, which is why they sit right after the individual-protection statutes rather than beside them. Wage-and-hour compliance and collective organizing then shift the frame from the individual relationship to the workplace collectively, one governing what every employee is owed regardless of any complaint, the other governing how employees can act together rather than alone. WARN Act notice extends that collective frame to its largest scale, an entire facility or workforce losing its jobs at once. Non-competes close the lecture precisely because they run against the grain of everything before them: where the first nine ideas are about protecting an employee during or after employment, the restrictive covenant is the one place the law lets an employer constrain what a departing employee can do next, and the sharp state-by-state variation in how far that constraint can go is the clearest reminder in this entire field that federal statutes do not cover everything, and that knowing which jurisdiction's law actually governs a given employment relationship is itself often the first and most consequential question.",
